{
 "id": "l01",
 "label": "1",
 "title": "The Tagalog alphabet",
 "summary": "Letters, vowels, the ng and r sounds, and how borrowed words are spelled",
 "minutes": 25,
 "goals": [
  "Know the letters of the alphabet and the five vowel sounds",
  "Make the special sounds ng, ñ and the tapped r",
  "Read the diphthongs ay, aw, ey, iw, oy and uy",
  "Decode Spanish and English loanwords in Tagalog spelling",
  "Learn the days of the week and months of the year"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "The letters",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog spelling is close to the way words sound. If you know the sounds of the letters, you can read almost any word aloud."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Around the 1930s the alphabet had **20 letters**: five vowels and fifteen consonants, with **ng** counted as one letter."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Group",
      "Letters"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Vowels (5)",
       "a, e, i, o, u"
      ],
      [
       "Consonants (15)",
       "b, k, d, g, h, l, m, n, ng, p, r, s, t, w, y"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "The older 20-letter alphabet"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "In the 1980s eight more letters were added: **c, f, j, ñ, q, v, x, z**. They are used mostly for names (Carlos, Jollibee), borrowed words and science terms. Today the letters are named as in English, except **ñ**, which is said *en-YE*."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Old and new",
     "t": "Older teaching names the consonants with an \"a\" after them (ba, ka, da). You may still hear this. Modern Tagalog uses English letter names."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "The five vowels",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Tagalog vowels are **short and pure**. English vowels often glide into a second sound (\"o\" becomes \"oh-oo\"). In Tagalog, say the vowel once and keep it steady."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Letter",
      "Sounds like",
      "Example word",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "a",
       "the a in father",
       "[[araw|noun]]  (A-raw)",
       "day, sun"
      ],
      [
       "e",
       "the e in bed",
       "[[bente|noun]]  (BEN-te)",
       "twenty"
      ],
      [
       "i",
       "the ee in feet, but shorter",
       "[[isa|noun]]  (i-SA)",
       "one"
      ],
      [
       "o",
       "the o in more, but pure",
       "[[oras|noun]]  (O-ras)",
       "hour, time"
      ],
      [
       "u",
       "the oo in food, but shorter",
       "[[uhaw|pred]]  (u-HAW)",
       "thirsty"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Vowel sounds"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "warn",
     "title": "Watch out",
     "t": "Do not shorten unstressed vowels into a vague \"uh\" the way English does. Each vowel keeps its own clear sound, even when it is not stressed."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Three special sounds",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "ng"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**ng** is one sound, like the end of the English word *sing*. The tricky part is that Tagalog also puts it at the **start** of words."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Try this: say *sing*. Hold the final \"ng\", then add a vowel straight after it: *ng-a*. Now say it without the \"si\" in front."
    },
    {
     "type": "examples",
     "items": [
      {
       "tl": "ngayon",
       "en": "now",
       "parts": [
        [
         "ngayon",
         "now (nga-YON)",
         "other"
        ]
       ]
      },
      {
       "tl": "Ang mga bata",
       "en": "The children",
       "parts": [
        [
         "Ang",
         "the",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "mga",
         "more than one (ma-NGA)",
         "marker"
        ],
        [
         "bata",
         "child",
         "noun"
        ]
       ]
      }
     ],
     "title": "Practise ng"
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "ñ"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**ñ** sounds like **n + y** said together. Santo Niño sounds like *nin-yo*."
    },
    {
     "type": "h",
     "t": "r"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The Tagalog **r** is a quick **tap** of the tongue tip against the ridge behind your upper teeth. It is the same flick as the \"tt\" in American *butter*. It is not the English r, and it is not a long rolled trill."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Practice words",
     "t": "[[araw|noun]], [[marami|pred]] (ma-RA-mi, many) and [[Pilipinas|noun]] all contain the tapped r."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Diphthongs: two vowels, one beat",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "A diphthong is a vowel glide that counts as **one syllable**. Tagalog has six: **ay, aw, ey, iw, oy, uy**."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Glide",
      "Example",
      "Meaning"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "ay",
       "[[away|noun]]  (A-way)",
       "a fight"
      ],
      [
       "aw",
       "[[araw|noun]]  (A-raw)",
       "day, sun"
      ],
      [
       "ey",
       "[[reyna|noun]]  (REY-na)",
       "queen"
      ],
      [
       "iw",
       "[[bitiw|pred]]  (BI-tiw)",
       "let go, release"
      ],
      [
       "oy",
       "[[amoy|noun]]  (a-MOY)",
       "scent"
      ],
      [
       "uy",
       "[[kasuy|noun]]  (ka-SUY)",
       "cashew"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "warn",
     "title": "The hard ones",
     "t": "English speakers often split **iw** and **uy** into two sounds at the end of a word. Say them as one smooth glide: bitiw, not bi-ti-oo."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Every syllable counts",
     "t": "Say each syllable clearly and separately. Do not swallow or blur any part of a word."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Borrowed words in Tagalog spelling",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "When a Spanish or English word enters Tagalog, it is respelled to match Tagalog sounds. If you learn the rules, you can decode a lot of vocabulary without a dictionary."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Original",
      "Becomes",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "hard c",
       "k",
       "café → [[kape|noun]] (coffee)"
      ],
      [
       "soft c",
       "s",
       "cine → [[sine|noun]] (cinema)"
      ],
      [
       "ch",
       "ts",
       "lechón → [[litson|noun]] (roast pig)"
      ],
      [
       "f",
       "p",
       "final → [[pinal|pred]]"
      ],
      [
       "j (Spanish)",
       "h or s",
       "cajón → [[kahon|noun]] (box), jabón → [[sabon|noun]] (soap)"
      ],
      [
       "ll",
       "ly",
       "calle → [[kalye|noun]] (street)"
      ],
      [
       "q (Spanish)",
       "k",
       "máquina → [[makina|noun]] (machine)"
      ],
      [
       "v",
       "b",
       "vapor → [[bapor|noun]] (ship)"
      ],
      [
       "z",
       "s",
       "lápiz → [[lapis|noun]] (pencil)"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "From Spanish"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Becomes",
      "Example"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "ck or ct",
       "k",
       "addict → [[adik|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "qu",
       "kw",
       "quality → [[kwaliti|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "long i",
       "ay",
       "driver → [[drayber|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "f",
       "p",
       "traffic → [[trapik|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "v",
       "b",
       "believe → [[bilib|pred]] (to believe, admire)"
      ],
      [
       "-tion",
       "-syon",
       "institution → [[institusyon|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "x",
       "ks",
       "boxing → [[boksing|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "j",
       "dy",
       "janitor → [[dyanitor|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "s + consonant at the start",
       "is + consonant",
       "school → [[iskul|noun]], sport → [[isport|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "soft ch",
       "ts",
       "teacher → [[titser|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "-cle",
       "-kel",
       "tricycle → [[traysikel|noun]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "From English"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Names stay as they are",
     "t": "People, places and brand names usually keep their original spelling: Carlos, Jollibee, Leyte Gulf. Only ordinary words are respelled."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Everyday loanwords",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "These borrowed words are used constantly. Learn to say them as true Tagalog words, with every vowel clear."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "borrowed",
     "title": "Common borrowed words"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Days and months",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The days of the week and the months of the year are Spanish in origin, respelled in Tagalog style."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "days",
     "title": "Days of the week"
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "months",
     "title": "Months of the year"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "The weekdays and months are easy to guess if you know a little Spanish.",
      "Linggo means both Sunday and week."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
